223:''The face key alone authors the history.'' One history stands per face key, so one per (PersonaGroup, Nexus). Each event carries the face key, the Nexus, its position, the head of the event before it, and a digest of the withdrawn vessel's face-scoped key. The face key signs it under the withdrawal's own domain. The first event names a genesis head derived from the face key and the Nexus. No quorum signs and no board carries the history: the Nexus admitted the face key once, and a vessel's certificate stands as a delegate beneath it.
225:''A denial names a key by digest.'' The digest binds the face key and the Nexus, so a gate that never met the vessel learns nothing of its key, and no two histories share a value. The denial covers every certificate the face key ever signs for that key. Every denial the face key signed counts wherever a reader verified it, whether or not the reader holds the events before it. ''A withdrawn key never returns'': a vessel that joins again mints a fresh key and takes a fresh certificate (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/kapae#/re-admission">>).
229:''The history travels with the face's own presenters, and with nobody else.'' Each vessel of the face brings the history to every gate it dials. A gate keeps the events of the faces that presented to it. It hands them to a presenter whose chain verified under that same face key, and to no other party: no board, no shelf and no other gate receives them from it. The gate keeps denials and never the certificates it saw, so it holds no inventory of a face's vessels (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/registry-filter#/the-test">>).
236:| `withdrawn` | a learned denial names the key | it refuses and hands over the denial | it stands withdrawn, says so, and leaves every channel of that face |
238:| `forked` | two different events the face key signed stand at one position, or an event names a predecessor the gate's line never held | it refuses every certificate under that face key, keeps the events that prove it, and keeps counting every denial on every branch | nothing clears it but a face-key roll (#/causal-withdrawal) |
241:''An answer that names a state goes only to a presenter whose three signatures verified under the admitted face key'', inside the encrypted channel and after the signed finish. That presenter holds a certificate the face key signed, so the answer tells it nothing a vessel of that face may not read. Every other failure draws the undifferentiated silence of #/the-gate-exchange.
247:''One face key's history holds a bounded count of events'', 1024 by the house's reading. The root holder signs no withdrawal past it and rolls the face key before it reaches it; a gate keeps no more for one face key and answers a longer history with silence. The bound caps what one admitted face can make a gate hold.
249:''The vessel's own reading follows the same history.'' A vessel folds the events it receives against its own key: it reads //standing//, //withdrawn// or //forked//, and between a gate's ask and its own fold it reads `unconfirmed` and acts on nothing shared.
255:''A gate that forgets a denial admits the withdrawn vessel again'', so what a sovereign gate learned outlives a restart. It rests in the gate's own vessel store, sealed under the vessel key behind the vessel's one store door, and on no board another operator reads.
257:* ''It holds signed events alone.'' Each row carries the withdrawal events one face key signed. A reader verifies every event again as it loads, so a row that holds one event the face key never signed teaches nothing.
259:* ''The gate writes a row only after a presentation under a held admit.'' The row itself proves who signed each event and never how it arrived: a hand that can write this vessel's sealed store can add an event the face key signed, and such an event denies only what that face key denied.
268:| a gate that learned nothing newer, facing the withdrawn vessel | that gate accepts the withdrawn vessel under the admitted face | the first honest vessel of that face to dial the gate; nothing else in this proof |
269:| a withdrawn vessel that shows an older frontier | nothing at a gate that holds the denial: the denial names its key whatever frontier it claims. At a gate that never learned, the row above | the same |
270:| a relay or a herm that withholds | nothing: the history rides inside the encrypted exchange between a presenter and the gate | |
273:| the root holder | no withdrawal gets signed at all (#/the-offline-root) | the root holder's return |
275:''A withheld withdrawal buys a delay and never a lift.'' The delay lasts at each gate until an honest vessel of the face dials it. The PersonaGroup's own rekey closes the private planes beside this proof and waits on no gate.
279:''Two acts remove a vessel, and each answers to its own readers.'' The dyad marker sets a device aside inside its fleet: it rides the fleet's edge board, closes the sibling lane and device sync, and moves the PersonaGroup's keys (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/kapae#/four-edges">>). The certificate withdrawal denies that vessel's face-scoped key at one Nexus's gates, and rides the face's own presentations. One removal signs the dyad raise once, and one withdrawal for each face key the vessel held a certificate under. The root-holding vessel signs both, each with one signature.
281:* ''Neither act rides a Nexus board.'' A gate reads the withdrawal and never the dyad; a sibling reads both.
282:* ''A dyad marker lowers; a withdrawal never lifts.'' A lowered dyad restores standing alone. The vessel then mints a fresh face-scoped key and takes a fresh certificate, as a lift restores standing and never keys.
283:* ''A standing dyad shadow with no withdrawal'' leaves the vessel shut out of its fleet and still accepted at a gate. A removal door therefore signs both acts or reports the one it could not sign.
287:''A key's bytes never say whether it served before.'' So the law rests with the two hands that sign. A joining vessel names every key it holds for anything else (its roots, its other faces' keys) and its request refuses over any of them before its key signs. The root holder names its own other face keys, and every vessel key it certified under another face, and its face key signs no certificate over any of them. The three keys of one chain read pairwise distinct at every reader.
291:''A session's certificate names its hand's profile, and the profile bounds the key.'' A session key signs under three purposes, each with its own domain: the dial's signed finish, an ordinary act, and an act that changes who holds authority. A person's tab and a person's CLI run sign all three. The disk watcher and a spirit's seat sign the first two, and no verifier reads an authority act under their key (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/session-contract#/the-hands">>). The watcher's row reads as the house's own: the ruling names the seat.
293:''A session opens before its certificate'' (operator ruling, approved as presented). The vessel's own chain verifies first: the face key, its certificate, this vessel's face-scoped key, with no learned denial. The face supplier joins THAT to the admit and the private grant, and the owner opens the session wearing the face. Only then does the signer mint the session's key and its certificate under the vessel's face-scoped key. The owner answers no caller between the two acts: one door opens the session and mints its signer, and a mint that fails closes the session it opened before any message routes to it. No session ever stands live with a face and no signer.
295:''A signer serves one session at one generation.'' It mints at random when the session opens, its certificate carries no session id and no owner epoch, and it drops when the session closes or switches. It asks the owner's own table before every signature and again before a signature leaves, so a record kept from before a close proves nothing and a close that lands mid-signature releases none.
297:''A session's face names one PersonaGroup at ONE Nexus'' (house reading). The proof subject and the admit both stand per Nexus, and the session contract's face carries one of each. A face that works at two Nexuses holds one signer for each (#/the-key-hierarchy); whether one session carries both or two sessions carry one each stays the session contract's word to give.
308:Keyhive keeps the PersonaGroup's private membership and its content keys. The face chain, the session signer and the withdrawal history stand as the house's own signed bytes.
316:''The sibling-facing proof moves to face scope together with the herm-facing stamp.'' The channel seals a face-scoped session proof and the face-private membership authority to its sibling, and binds its Repo peer id to the proven face session. It transmits no device delegation and derives no peer id from a device key. The herm stamps the separate per-dial relay identity alone.
330:* a joined vessel keeps using the certificate it holds, under the face key the Nexus already admitted;
331:* ''nobody new joins'': no certificate issues, and a wiped device cannot return;
332:* ''nobody leaves at a gate'': the face key signs every withdrawal, so no withdrawal gets signed, and a vessel set aside keeps the certificate a gate accepts;
347:* ''The subjects'' (`packages/lararium-mesh/src/face-proof-subjects.ts`): the typed keys, the scope, the certificate and its request, the withdrawal event, the frontier, the session certificate, every signed image, and a reader for each. Every key and signature passes the one door (`ed25519-door.ts`); the purposes stand in the domain table (`domains.ts`).
348:* ''Private authority'' (`packages/lararium-mesh/src/face-authority.ts`): minting, the request, the certificate, the withdrawal, the fold of a reader's memory, and the chain check with its six verdicts. Its callers: the fleet-join door, the removal door and the gate. It also reads a vessel's OWN chain with no session key in it (`checkVesselChain`), which a session opens on.
350:* ''The gate bundle'' (`packages/lararium-mesh/src/gate-bundle.ts`): the floor and sovereign tiers with their typed routes and roots (the serving tier stands owed with the ember's train E), the pin, its digest, the knock, the prologue, and the transport and signing keys one gate derives per (tier, Nexus, route). Nothing rests. Its caller: the vessel's gate composer.
352:* ''The gate'' (`packages/lararium-mesh/src/face-gate.ts`): the whole exchange against a socket and the collaborators it asks: a deadline source, an admit reader over the one presenter-verifier, the shadow ring, the grant ring, the sorter, a withdrawal memory and a session table. Every silence names ONE reason from the gate's fixed vocabulary on the vessel's own log (`GATE_SILENCE_REASONS`), with at most one detail drawn from a vocabulary the gate already types; no thrown error's text and no string a collaborator spelled reaches that log. A first flight of any length but 96 bytes draws silence before any Noise work. Its caller: the socket cutover.
354:* ''The gate's stored memory'' (`packages/lararium-mesh/src/gate-withdrawal-store.ts`): one row of signed events per face key, verified again at every load, accreting under one writer. Its caller: the gate composer, over rows the vessel seals.
355:* ''The session face supplier'' (`packages/lararium-mesh/src/session-face.ts`): it joins a verified chain, this vessel's own key for the face, the admit's verdict and the private grant into the face a session wears, and verifies none of the last three itself (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/session-contract#/what-a-session-carries">>). Its callers: the same two.
359:* ''Two custody rows.'' The face proof key rests cold with the root holder, one per (PersonaGroup, Nexus); a vessel's face-scoped key rests hot on its own vessel, one per (PersonaGroup, Nexus). Each holds 32 secret bytes and reads whole or refuses by name. The modules name the rows and the two signing doors they ask of the custody seat, and write no row.
360:* ''The admit's own mark.'' The supplier reads the presenter-verifier's verdict over an admit and checks it against the chain. The admit's quorum signs a commitment to what it carries (`carriageCommit`), and the held-root door hands back the head of the admitted nym's key line, which the supplier holds against the chain's face key; the verdict itself still carries no mark a caller cannot spell by hand. A chain another vessel presented verifies as well as this vessel's own, so the session opener also owes the supplier the key its own custody row names.
362:* ''Where a face's own history rests on its vessels'': in the face's private causal state. A gate's own memory rests in its vessel store (#/the-withdrawal-proof).
364:* ''Sealed rows for the gate's memory'' stand at the custody seat, and no gate calls them until the socket cutover. The store asks a door for one row of bytes by name (`gateMemoryRows`, `packages/lararium-node/src/gate-memory-rows.ts`): one row per face key in the vessel's storage home, named by the proof-subject digest, sealed under the vessel key through the seat's one-holder update door. One holder answers one door, so the gates of one composition share one writer; and a write that lacks an event the row holds refuses while the row keeps its bytes. A locked vessel reads none and refuses by name; a row that stands and does not open, or opens to anything but a list of events, refuses at the read and at the write, its bytes never move, and the gate that asked falls silent. The store drops its view of a row whose write the door refused and verifies that row again. The seat holds no counter a restored disk could not also restore, so the door adds no fence against a rollback (#/the-withdrawal-proof). The row belongs to its vessel, and no face's backup bundle carries it. The composer owes the call.
365:* ''A held Nexus root per gate'': the roster, the charter lineage, the deny board and the antigen that the admit reader folds, with the causal position it read them at. A gate that holds no root refuses every admit by name.
367:* ''The sorter over proven facts'': a class from a held admit, a private grant, the fleet reading and the arm as presented, over ONE Nexus's posture.
373:* ''The withdrawal proof under a live partition.'' The fold and the six verdicts stand proven as pure functions over synthetic histories, and the six answers stand proven at one gate against a dialer in the same process, partitioned by hand. No two gates and no real herm have yet carried one.
382:# ''Sibling carriage.'' The herm dial, the inner proof and the sibling peer id move to face scope.

