    76	The dialer pins the gate bundle before it dials. Every signature uses its own domain bytes and a canonical field encoding.
    77	
    78	# ''The first flight.'' A browser's upgrade request carries the knock in its URL path and a ''minimal'' Noise IK message 1 as a token in `Sec-WebSocket-Protocol`, beside one fixed application protocol name. A node dialer sends raw Noise and no upgrade.
    79	# ''The gate answers the upgrade only after that flight decrypts.'' A flight that decrypts shows the dialer knows the gate's pinned key, which silence toward strangers protects. The gate then sends its 101 and selects the fixed protocol name alone. A wrong knock or a flight that fails to decrypt draws no answer.
    80	# ''The first flight buys provisional work and nothing more.'' IK's first flight can replay, so the gate performs no application operation on its strength.
    81	# ''The gate completes the handshake.'' Its reply carries no verdict.
    82	# ''The presentation follows in the first encrypted frames.'' A face's presentation carries the face's public key, the vessel's face-scoped key with its certificate, the session key with its certificate, ONE presentation arm (an admit, a hosting grant, or an invite claim) and that arm's causal lineage, and the presenter's withdrawal frontier with any events the gate may lack (#/the-withdrawal-proof). An admit's `nym` MUST equal the face key. No ContactCard, no vessel key, no persona root, no root edge, no identifier shared across faces, and by default no Handle rides it.
    83	
    84	''The admit's signed bytes carry the canon-document list and a validated full `GateBundlePin` for the hearth's sovereign sync route at its named Nexus. `readGateBundlePin` reads the object and its digest derives from it; the quorum's salted `carriageCommit` binds the opening inside the act image. A joinee reads the opening only through the admit at its held root (`verifySignedAdmitBundle`). Floor routes receive separate full pins through an authenticated relation or signed pin set; a floor announcement cannot authenticate its own first pin. `device-admit.ts` mints the internal `device-admit/v1` edge, and the fleet answer carries the pin and canon list.
    85	
    86	# ''The gate checks, still provisionally.'' It checks the pinned route and audience, the chain, the presentation's signatures, the quorum's signatures, the causal parents, the deny events and withdrawals it has learned (#/causal-withdrawal), and the roll anchors. Where the asked planes need it, the ring checks the PersonaGroup grant. Missing required history, a known fork or an incomplete board brings abstention. The gate reveals no differentiated refusal.
    87	# ''The signed finish.'' The session signer signs the COMPLETED handshake hash, the gate bundle's digest, the face key, the presentation, the audience and the session key. A recorder of the first flight lacks the dial's secrets and cannot produce it.
    88	# ''The verdict comes after the finish, and never before.'' The gate verifies the finish and returns its signed verdict inside the encrypted channel. Only a `verified` chain attaches the socket to application sync (#/the-withdrawal-proof).
    89	# ''Both ends close the dial'' and discard its transport and herm-dial keys. The daemon drops the session signer when its session ends or switches.
    90	
    91	''What retires together, in one cutover'': the `lar:challenge` frame, the ContactCard proof, and every gate proof under a vessel key. No second wire protocol runs beside this one.
    92	
    93	> ''House readings, marked as such; the operator may overturn each.'' The gate and the dialer that stand specified in code (#/owed) read the exchange above this way.
    94	
    95	* ''The knock derives from the bundle's digest'', and the handshake's prologue holds that digest. So a gate's knock, its pin, its verdict and its posture all read per Nexus exactly, and a first flight decrypts only at the gate whose pin its dialer held.
